/*!
    \example extensions/volumetricfog
    \ingroup quick3d-examples
    \title Qt Quick 3D - Volumetric Fog Extension Example
    \examplecategory {3D}
    \brief Demonstrates how to implement volumetric fog in a render extension.
    \image volumetric-fog-example.webp
           {Bare tree on a round tiered stage lit by red, green, and blue
           volumetric light beams, with a settings panel for lights, fog,
           and shadows}

    \section1 Overview

    This example implements volumetric fog as a Qt Quick 3D
    \l{QQuick3DRenderExtension}{render extension}, and shows how to reuse the
    extension in your own project.

    The extension divides the camera frustum into a 3D grid of \e{froxels},
    frustum-aligned voxels, and uses a compute shader to accumulate the light
    that scatters toward the camera in each cell. A post-processing effect then
    composites the result onto the rendered frame.

    \section1 Controls

    Drag with the mouse to orbit the camera around the scene. Use the settings
    panel to change the lighting, the fog, the post-processing, and the shadow
    settings.

    \section1 Implementation

    \note The extension injects light into the froxel grid with a compute
          shader, so it requires a graphics API and hardware with compute
          shader support. OpenGL versions older than 4.3 and OpenGL ES
          versions older than 3.1 have no compute support. See
          \l QRhi::Compute for details.

    The \c VolumetricFogExtension type implements the volumetric fog extension.
    It lives in the separate \c VFExtension subproject, so you can add it to
    your own project unchanged.

    Set the following properties on \c VolumetricFogExtension:

    \snippet extensions/volumetricfog/Main.qml fog extension

    The \c froxelWidth, \c froxelHeight, and \c froxelDepth properties define
    the resolution of the froxel grid that the compute shader fills. A higher
    resolution produces more detailed light scattering, but uses more GPU
    memory and compute time.

    The \c nearPlane and \c farPlane properties define the depth range that the
    volumetric effect covers. Match them to the clip distances of the
    \l PerspectiveCamera.

    The \c fogVolumes property holds the list of \c Fog3DVolume nodes that
    define where fog is present in the scene.

    \section2 IES Light Profiles

    A photometric profile from the Illuminating Engineering Society (IES)
    shapes the angular light distribution of a spot light. The \c iesTexture
    property takes a \l Texture whose data comes from an \c IESTextureData
    object. That object parses \c{.ies} files and packs them into a 2D atlas
    texture. The \c iesCount property specifies how many profiles the atlas
    holds, and \c iesLightProfiles maps individual lights to profile indices
    through \c IESLightProfileIndex objects.

    \snippet extensions/volumetricfog/Main.qml ies texture

    \section2 Attaching the Extension to a View3D

    Add the extension to the \l{View3D::extensions}{extensions} list of the
    \l View3D. The render pipeline then invokes the extension's compute shader
    once per frame:

    \qml
        View3D {
            extensions: [ froxelExtension ]
        }
    \endqml

    \section2 Fog Volumes

    A \c Fog3DVolume node defines a fog region in the scene. The \c type
    property selects one of two shapes, \c{Fog3DVolume.Sphere} or
    \c{Fog3DVolume.Box}, and the \c extents property sets the bounding
    dimensions. The \c color and \c density properties control the appearance.

    Advancing \c noiseOffset each frame animates the noise. To make the density
    fall off with height, set \c heightEnabled to \c true and adjust
    \c leastIntenseY, \c mostIntenseY, and \c heightCurve:

    \snippet extensions/volumetricfog/Main.qml fog volume

    \section2 VolumetricFogEffect

    The extension exposes its result through the read-only \c froxelTexture
    property, a 3D RGBA texture that holds the accumulated light scattering
    data for each froxel. Add the \c VolumetricFogEffect post-processing effect
    to the \l ExtendedSceneEnvironment to consume that texture. The effect
    ray-marches through the froxel texture and composites the volumetric light
    onto the rendered frame:

    \snippet extensions/volumetricfog/Main.qml fog effect

    The \c marchSteps property controls the number of ray marching steps. Match
    it to \c froxelDepth. The \c frameBaseJitter and \c jitterIntensity
    properties enable temporal dithering, which reduces banding artifacts when
    the scene also uses temporal antialiasing.

    \section1 Reusing the Extension in Your Own Project

    The \c VFExtension subproject builds as a QML module of its own. To use it
    in another project, copy the directory, add it with
    \c{add_subdirectory()}, and link the library into your target:

    \badcode
    add_subdirectory(VFExtension)

    target_link_libraries(myapp PRIVATE VFExtension)
    \endcode

    Then import the module in QML:

    \qml
        import VFExtension
    \endqml

    \note The extension includes private Qt Quick 3D headers and links against
          \c Qt6::Quick3DPrivate. Private API carries no source or binary
          compatibility guarantee, so the extension may need changes when you
          move to a newer Qt version.

    \sa {Qt Quick 3D - Stencil Outline Extension Example},
        {Qt Quick 3D - Simple Fog Example},
        {User-Defined Render Passes in Qt Quick 3D}
*/
